{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 28, :comments ["<b>The hidden things belong to HaShem our Elohim, and the revealed things belong to us and to our children.</b> Rashi, of blessed memory, wrote: [the scribe] placed dots over לנו ולבנינו and over the ע of עד, to interpret that even for the revealed things He did not punish the community until they crossed the Jordan, once they accepted the oath upon themselves at Mount Gerizim and Mount Ebal and became guarantors for one another. And he cites the Yerushalmi which said thus: Rabbi Shimon ben Lakish said, 'At the Jordan they accepted the hidden things upon themselves. Joshua said to them: If you do not accept the hidden things upon yourselves, the waters will come and sweep you away.' Rabbi Shimon ben Zavida said: 'How do you know this? For Achan sinned and the majority of the Sanhedrin fell at Ai.' A bat kol issued and said to them: 'In you the strap was loosened — you have no business with hidden things.' And the meaning of 'in you the strap was loosened' is that you are now being punished for the hidden things. And some interpreted 'in you the strap was loosened' as referring to when Moses said 'in you' — that is, פן יש בכם — then the strap was loosened to be punished for the hidden things,'" "<b>And Rashi</b> explained there in tractate Sanhedrin that once they accepted the oath upon themselves they became liable for the hidden things, and it would have been fitting to place dots over לה' אלהינו, in order to interpret that the hidden things do not belong to HaShem alone, for they also belong to us; but since it is not the honor of the Name to place dots over it, [the scribe] placed dots over לנו ולבנינו in its place. Therefore it was necessary to place a dot over the עי״ן corresponding to the letters of לה' אלהינו, to complete 11 letters." "<b>And the Ramban,</b> of blessed memory, wrote: My opinion by way of the plain sense (peshat) is that the hidden things are the sins hidden from those who commit them, as in (Psalms 19:13) שגיאות מי יבין מנסתרות נקני — that is, the hidden things belong to HaShem alone; there is in them no offense for us that one has sinned. But the revealed things — which are the deliberate sins — belong to us and to our children forever, to do all the words of this Torah as an everlasting statute; for thus we have accepted upon those who are not here for all generations. And since he brought Israel by means of the curses to do all the commandments, he excluded from the ban the one who acts unwittingly, that he not be cursed in this curse — end quote." "<b>And the sage</b> Rabbi Abraham, of blessed memory, explained: 'The hidden things' — one who worships idolatry in secret, his judgment is in the hand of the Blessed Name and He will exact punishment from him; there is upon us no obligation in it." "<b>And I heard</b> in the name of the Rambam, of blessed memory, the interpretation of this verse: הנסתרות לה' אלהינו — that is, the secrets of the Torah that are hidden, and the reason for the commandments, belong to Him, may He be exalted. And if a person merits that his ear catches a whisper of them in knowing the root of the commandment and its principle in the secret within it, let him not thereby exempt himself from the revealed aspect, so as not to perform the commandment in its physical form — he has no license to refrain from this, for the revealed things belong to us and to our children, to do. And this interpretation itself is precious and precious (sic), but it is not in keeping with the subject of the passage." "<b>It is possible</b> to interpret 'the secret things belong to YHWH our Elohim' as follows: because in the preceding verse He mentioned 'and He uprooted them and cast them into another land, as on this day,' and the exile of the banished and the scattered is exceedingly long, and the end is sealed and hidden and secret, therefore He immediately appended and said, 'the secret things belong to YHWH our Elohim' — that is, we have no knowledge of it whatsoever, for it is a matter hidden to YHWH our Elohim alone, and there is none among us who knows to what extent; 'but the revealed things belong to us and to our children' — that is, when those hidden things become revealed to us and to our children, we are obligated to do all the words of this Torah. And this verse teaches us that our Torah endures forever; it will never be annulled in any time, not even in the time of the Messiah, and the observance of the Torah of Moses is equal in every time — both in the time of the hidden things, which is the exile, and in the time of the revealed things, which is the redemption. And from this the Sages, blessed be their memory, expounded (Deuteronomy 16:3): 'ימי חייך' — 'the days of your life' — this refers to this world; 'כל ימי חייך' — 'all the days of your life' — to bring the days of the Messiah. And with this interpretation the verse will be properly fitted and linked with what He mentioned in the passage above and below." "<b>And you should be roused to ask</b> concerning the word הנסתרת — that it is written deficient, without a vav: 'הנסתרת כתיב' (it is written הנסתרת). And the verse alludes to the Shekhinah. And the reason it called it הנסתרת, 'the hidden one,' is because it would hide itself in a cloud; and it is הנסתרת, hidden from us, in the time of the exile in which we are, and in the concealment of the face — as it is written (Deuteronomy 32): 'אסתירה פני מהם.' And the verse says that הנסתרת, the hidden one, seeks mercy from HaShem our Elohim, that He may reveal the end to us and to our children. And likewise we find several verses that require redemption for themselves, and thus the Sages, blessed be their memory, expounded (Psalms 9:15): 'אגילה בישועתך' — it was not said 'in our salvation' but 'בישועתך,' 'in Your salvation'; and likewise (Psalms 60): 'הושיעה ימינך וענני'; and likewise it is written (II Samuel 7:23): 'אשר פדית לך ממצרים גוים ואלהיו.' And consider the words of the prophet, peace be upon him, who said (Isaiah 30:18): 'ולכן יחכה ה׳ לחננכם ולכן ירום לרחמכם' — and I have already drawn your attention to the secret of this verse in this work. And you should gain further insight, that from this verse there emerges a proven name for the petition of a dream through the combination of letters, and they are three names, each and every name of three letters, and all of them are nine, and it is the secret of the doubled טי״ת in the known Name; and this name testifies that there is no vav in the word הנסתרות — however, we found the vav of אלהינו which remained solitary from the combination, and this is clear."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 29, :notes {6 "The second citation to 'שם ס' (Psalms 60) is given without a verse number by Rabbeinu Bahya; the quoted phrase 'הושיעה ימינך וענני' does not correspond to any verse in Psalms 60 and may be corrupt or refer to a different passage."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:52:24.394563920Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}